Christiane's trace their origins to a  Jew named Jesus who lived and preached  about the kingdom of God under the Roman  Empire in the 1st century CE II Jesus  left no writings but his followers wrote  many Gospels letters and apocalyptic  books that claim he is the son of God  and the promised Jewish Messiah the  Christ Christians also accept a  collection of Jewish Scriptures  containing divine law prophecy songs  prayers love poetry and narratives of  God's actions these scriptures teach  that God created the world and humanity  and goodness yet the first humans Adam  and Eve disobeyed God he punished them  with suffering and death but he also  showed great mercy God chose Israel to  be his people and a light to the  nation's  he gave the divine law to Moses and sent  prophets to call people to righteousness  and joy Christian's appropriated and  interpreted these Jewish writings as  prophecy of the coming of the Messiah  Jesus letters written by Paul and his  companions taught that Jesus's death and  resurrection fulfilled God's plan for  the eternal salvation  later followers wrote stories of Jesus's  life and teachings in books called  Gospels they proclaimed Jesus as the  co-creator of the world the child of a  virgin named Mary a divine prophet  teacher healer and miracle worker  he called on people to repent and turn  to the kingdom of God the greatest  Commandments he taught are to love God  with one's whole heart mind and strength  and to love one's neighbor as oneself in  the end he was caught up in local  political tensions and crucified by the  Romans outside of Jerusalem the Bible  teaches that God raised him from the  dead and he appeared to his disciples  before his final ascent to God  commissioning them to spread his  teachings to the ends of the earth it  claims he will return at the end of time  and usher in the final judgment granting  eternal bliss to believers and eternal  torment to all others the apocalypse  tells of the final battles and the  creation of a new heaven and our  from its inception at the crossroads of  three continents Asia Africa and Europe  Christianity spread around the  Mediterranean Sea north to Germany and  Britain west from Greece to Spain from  Egypt along the African coast and south  to Ethiopia east through Syria to India  and beyond in 16th century Europe a set  of movements tied to new political and  social developments assured in the  Protestant Reformation at the same time  Christian missionaries accompanied  European expansion to every corner of  the globe following the tracks of empire  trade and colonization European  Christians played many roles sometimes  complicit in the exploitation and  oppression of colonized peoples  sometimes colonialism's strongest  critics sometimes themselves fleeing  religious violence and seeking to  establish safe places to practice their  faith new forms of Christian belief and  practice were established in North and  South America throughout Asia Australia  and the Pacific shaped by local  languages cultures and histories  although this diversity is often  represented by religious leaders and  theologians or described in terms of its  main institutional or theological forms  Roman Catholicism Eastern Orthodoxy  Protestantism and Pentecostalism they  don't tell the whole story the lived  practice of Christians includes local  forms of art architecture and music  celebrating holidays and rituals  practices of personal prayer and  devotion people of all classes races  genders and walks of life comprise the  richness of Christianity  [Music]
 

